		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1961">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://perl.gamespot.com/dream/rpg/swords/index.html" target="_new">Draconus Cult of the Wyrm Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			March&nbsp;2, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">Videogames.com lets us get a sneak peek at the upcoming RPG for the Dreamcast. Here is some of what they found:<p>
"In true cliched marketing spiel, your job is to embark on an epic quest to unite the shattered kingdoms of humanity while saving them from the evil clutches of the rampaging dragon. To accomplish this feat, two characters are provided for the game player to play. Cynric is a muscle-bound knight who has superior hand-to-hand combat capabilities, while Aeowyn is a beautiful indomitable sorceress who, not surprisingly, prefers magic to defeat her enemies."<p>
Use the link to read the full preview.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1960">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://dreamcast.planetweb.com/gamers/gurureview.html?game=streetfight3rd" target="_new">Street Fighter III: Third Strike Review</a>
			
				&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="screenshots.asp?PID=1148&PNum=0&PMax=6" class="NV2">Screenshots</a>
				
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			March&nbsp;2, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">PlanetWeb reviewer gives us his opinion of one of the latest fighting games to be made for the Dreamcast console. Here is some of what he had to say:<p>
"Compared to the previous Street Fighter titles released on Dreamcast, this one does not stack up well. There were no advancements made in the game, and graphically there was a downgrade. With Street Fighter titles, most gamers expect to see some improvements each time a new one is released, but simply expanding the number of characters is not sufficient. All this does is makes the game a little harder to beat because you have to go through more opponents. There is nothing about Street fighter III that makes it stand out, and it can be considered a failed effort. Despite everything that was wrong with the game, Street Fighter fanatics will still enjoy it simply because it’s Street Fighter."<p>
<b>Overall:</b> 7<p>
Use the link for the full review and some screenshots.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1959">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.alldreamcast.com/games/halflife/preview.shtml" target="_new">Half-Life Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			March&nbsp;2, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">AllDreamcast.com let's us get an advanced look at the new action game coming out for the Dreamcast. Here is a sample of what they found out about the game:<p>
"Half-Life combines great storytelling with intense action and advanced technology to create an impressive, realistic world where players need to think smart to survive. Originally created for the PC by Valve, Half-Life has won more than 50 Game of the Year awards from publications around the world, was named "Best PC Game Ever" by Imagine Media's PC Gamer magazine (November 1999), and has sold over 1.5 million copies worldwide.<p>
Now, in development by Northern California-based Captivation Digital Laboratories in conjunction with Gearbox Software and Valve, Half-Life: Dreamcast is set to be even more extraordinary than the original PC version. The Dreamcast edition will feature several optimizations, including higher polygon player characters and dramatic lighting and special effects, to showcase the power of the Dreamcast and deliver a fresh and exciting player experience for console gamers."<p>
Use the link for the full exciting preview by AllDreamcast.com along with some screenshots.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1958">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.alldreamcast.com/games/oogabooga/preview.shtml" target="_new">Ooga Booga Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			March&nbsp;2, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">AllDreamcast.com gives a preview of Ooga Booga. Yes that is the title of the new game. Here is what they had to say:<p>
"The game's title is borrowed from one of its characters, the volcano goddess Ooga Booga. Players will select from one of four tribal witch doctors, called Kahunas, each having their own unique powers and characteristics. The object of the game is to power up your Kahuna's mojo by participating in various tribal gaming events which are designed to appease the goddess Ooga Booga. Hidden islands, more spells, and additional characters will be unlocked as the game progresses.<p>
Ooga Booga will primarily be an online game, but will feature a story-based offline version as well. The best news of all is that it's being developed by Visual Concepts, the development team behind most of the Sega Sports games, including NFL 2K1 and NBA 2K1, as well as the upcoming Floigan Bros. Sounds like Sega is really putting these guys to work."<p>
Click on the link to get the full preview.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1957">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.alldreamcast.com/games/kao/review.shtml" target="_new">Kao the Kangaroo Review</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;28, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">AllDreamcast.com let's us know their impressions of the new boxing kangaroo. Is this game for you? Let's find out by seeing some of what they had to say about the game:<p>
"Normally, there's nothing wrong with delivering the same type of game we're used to, especially if it's done right. Not every game needs to be revolutionary. Kao could have been much better if more effort was put into it. There's no reason for it to be so choppy when better looking games run smooth. The developers could have tightened up the camera. They could have easily fixed the control problems. They could have, but they didn't. Even the mediocre Super Magnetic Neo had more effort put into it than this. On a system that already has Rayman 2 and Sonic Adventure, and with Sonic Adventure 2 on it's way, Kao The Kangaroo is destined to be forgotten. Don't waste your time with this one."<p> 
<b>Kao The Kangaroo - The Scores:</b><br> 
<b>Final:</b> 5.5<br>
<b>Graphics:</b> 7.5<br>
<b>Sound:</b> 7.0<br>
<b>Gameplay:</b> 6.0<br>
<b>Value:</b> 6.5<p>
Click on the link for the full review and some screenshots.   </font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1956">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.zdnet.com/gamespot/filters/products/0,11114,468716,00.html" target="_new">Conflict Zone Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;28, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">ZDNet Gamespot gives us a look at the upcoming game Conflict Zone. Will this game be something worth looking into? Here is some of what they had to say:<p>
"Getting down to the nuts and bolts of gameplay, Conflict Zone has unique campaigns for both the ICP and dictators, and each campaign spans eight missions. Despite the game's slightly futuristic setting, it features mostly modern-day weapons, with various types of helicopters, tanks, APCs, and infantry for both sides. Most units have multiple abilities--infantry units, for example, can camouflage themselves quickly in the terrain, setting up ambushes or fortifying positions, and some helicopters can both attack enemies and carry troops or small equipment. The levels are extremely large, spanning as many as 20 kilometers in any direction. The game also features Internet support for up to four players simultaneously on SegaNet.<p>
Of course, Conflict Zone won't be able to make a name for itself on its graphics alone. Moreover, the game's subject matter and genre are nothing that we haven't seen before. This all means that Masa's focus on "real-world" combat--the sociopolitical repercussions of media and warfare--will be the biggest factor in whether or not Conflict Zone finds an audience on the DC. In that respect, the game has potential (and at least looks unique), since this real-world context and its repercussions on the game are all but ignored in conventional RTS games that are set in fictional versions of Earth, or in fairy-tale lands. Look for Conflict Zone to arrive on Dreamcasts this summer."<p>
Use the link for the full preview.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1955">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.segaweb.com/reviews/dreamcast/ironaces.html" target="_new">Iron Aces Review</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;28, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">Segaweb gives us their impressions of the game Iron Aces. Here is what they had to say:<p>
"When all is said and done, Iron Aces proves itself as a solid game, and one of the best combat flight sims available on the console. The title might be a bit light on the history and heavy on action (even if it is somewhat repetitive), but its sheer playability more than makes up for the lack."<p>
<b>Graphics:</b> 7.5<br>
<b>Sound:</b> 7.0<br>
<b>Gameplay:</b> 7.0<br>
<b>Depth:</b> 6.5<br>
<b>Presentation:</b> 6.0<br>
<b>Overall:</b> 7.0<br>
<b>The Final Word:</b> "It may be one of a small group of flying games on the DC, but it stands out as one of the best, and well worth the cash if you like a good dose of aerial action in your gaming diet. The cookie-cutter missions, and standard presentation hurt the game, but they certainly don't make it unplayable. Solid title."<p>
Click on the link for the full review and some screenshots.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1954">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.dreamcast.net/review.cfm?review_id=171" target="_new">Kao the Kangaroo Review</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;28, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">Dreamcast.net did a review on Kao the Kangaroo. Here is some of what they found out about the game:<p>
<b>Bottomline:</b><br>
"Definitely get this game if you are platform gamer and if you are under 12. For the rest that don't fall into that category, I suggest renting it before buying it just to test and feel it out...then if you like it - go ahead and buy it and if you are picky like me, pass it up."<p> 
<b>The Scores:</b><br>
<b>Gameplay:</b> Fair<br>
<b>Visuals:</b> Fair<br>
<b>Aurals:</b> Good<br> 
<b>Solo Play:</b> Good<br>
<b>Multi-Player:</b> Excellent<br>
<b>Fun Factor:</b> Good<br> 
<b>Overall Rating:</b> 76%<p>
Use the link for the full review. </font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1953">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.segadojo.com/previews/index.cgi?id=220" target="_new">Heroes of Might & Magic III Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;25, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">SegaDojo gives us a first look at the PC classic adapted for the Dreamcast. Here is a sample of what you could expect according to SegaDojo:<p>
"While the PC version includes fantastic multi-player support with up to eight players getting in on the fun, the Dreamcast rendition is sticking solely into the realms of single player. This is a bit disheartening at first, but when you think about it, strategy games are usually meant for the long gaming sessions where you just want a calm, restful time - and the game will without a doubt deliver that.<p>
Heroes of Might and Magic III should be released in the first week of June, just in time for long, lazy summer sessions of gaming. If it can deliver the top-notch turn based strategy action with delightful fantasy setting it's promising, then we're all in for an incredible treat from 3DO and Ubi Soft. And from we've seen from the PC version, we haven't got a single reason to not expect all of our wishes to come true!"<p>
Use the link for screenshots along with the full preview.</font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1952">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.dcswirl.com/reviews/rev_namcomuseum.shtml" target="_new">Namco Museum Review</a>
			
				&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="screenshots.asp?PID=1713&PNum=0&PMax=6" class="NV2">Screenshots</a>
				
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;25, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">Will the arcade classics continue to live on using the Dreamcast platform? Let's see what DC Swirl had to say when they did their review:<p>
"It's hard to screw-up something as simple as bringing back old games on a console as powerful as the Dreamcast, but that's exactly what Namco and Mass Media have done with the only compilation from Namco classics ever released on a Sega console ("Ms. Pac-Man Maze Madness" is more of a remake than a compilation, even though "Ms. Pac-Man" is pefectly emulated in that game). If you love "Dig-Dug", "Galaga", "Galaxian", "Ms. Pac-Man", "Pac-Man" and/or "Pole Position", do their memory and lasting legacy justice by ignoring the bastardized and underwhelming way in which Namco has packaged them on this GD-ROM, and look for them as cheap "Namco's Museum" PSX Greatest Hits CD's worth owning. You'll thank me when you get to play TWO versions of "Pole Position", TWO versions of "Rally-X" (with a neat BGM tune to boot) and a whole museum full of interesting stuff to look at."<p>
<b>Overall:</b> 2/5<p>
Click on the link for the full review. </font>
			<br><br><a class="NV2" href="#Top">Top</a>
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>&nbsp;</td>
	</tr>

	<tr>
		<td width="470">
			<a NAME="News1951">
			
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2"><b><a class="NV2" href="http://www.dailyradar.com/previews/game_preview_1303.html" target="_new">Crazy Taxi 2 Preview</a>
			
			</b></font></td>
			
	</tr> 
	<tr>
		<td>
			<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
			February&nbsp;25, 2001<p>
			<font size="1">DailyRadar gives us a first look at Crazy Taxi 2. Let's see what we can expect from the sequel to the already popular Crazy Taxi:<p>
"Making the jump from West Coast to East means introducing a new cast of characters, too. Whether the game's designers will be including any of the curiously stereotypical characterizations from the first game remains to be seen, but we're quite certain that each of these drivers will be suitably crazy -- which is the only way to drive in New York. Replacing Axl and the gang are Cinnamon, Hot-D, Iceman and Slash. Cinnamon's a sassy urbanite whose ride is a big, orange cruiser. Hot-D is the suicidal type (even for a cabbie) -- his cab's a classic, but there's a real hot rod engine under the hood. Iceman is the classiest of the crew; his cab is decked out for luxury, and his body's decked out with tribal tattoos. Slash is your standard, straight-up standby; his car is standard and he's purportedly both cool and crazy -- which seems to be a standard in the Crazy Taxi universe.<p>
Few other details have been released, other than that The Offspring will once again contribute to the soundtrack, and that the Methods of Mayhem will also be chipping in. Any gamer who's lost a good chunk of change and time to the original knows that as long as the addictive arcade action from first has been maintained, it's sure to be a smash."<p>
Use the link provided for the full preview and some great screenshots.
</font>
